WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:01.699
Ding-dong!

00:00:01.699 --> 00:00:03.379
Michael's gone -- but is he, really?

00:00:03.379 --> 00:00:05.009
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:05.009 --> 00:00:08.750
While Laurie gives a brief overview of what
happened to her, Allyson, and the rest of

00:00:08.750 --> 00:00:13.210
Haddonfield after the events of "Halloween
Kills," there are still some things left hanging.

00:00:13.210 --> 00:00:17.460
Sure, Laurie talks about her and Allyson now
living together and having a bit of a dysfunctional

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:21.779
relationship, as well as Haddonfield becoming
consumed by the grief and anger from Michael's

00:00:21.779 --> 00:00:24.080
killing spree and sudden disappearance.

00:00:24.080 --> 00:00:28.820
But the new film sort of just glosses over
some pretty big lingering emotions and story

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:30.590
threads from the previous movie.

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:34.580
For instance, what were the consequences of
the town causing one of the escaped mental

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.489
patients to fall out of the hospital to his
death?

00:00:37.489 --> 00:00:41.640
Also, throughout both previous films, both
Laurie and Allyson were ready to have Michael's

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:46.430
head on a silver platter and now they just
suddenly decide to stop after he disappears?

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:50.500
Even the absolute massacre that Michael enacts
at the end of "Halloween Kills" barely gets

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:54.989
any mention or follow-up, leaving the devastation
of it with no fulfilling resolution.

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:59.210
Truthfully, "Halloween Ends" doesn't transition
well from "Halloween Kills" and makes a lot

00:00:59.210 --> 00:01:04.080
of what was built up during that film's wild
finale have no lasting impact.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:08.200
Michael Myers is no stranger to disappearing
acts, since he can literally vanish in a flash

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.700
if you don't keep your eyes on him.

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.810
However, it's a little odd that Michael is
just suddenly gone from Haddonfield without

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:17.330
a trace after "Halloween Kills," especially
since he was winning.

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:21.380
He was ripping people to shreds on the streets
of Haddonfield and even got to kill Karen

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.340
just to twist the knife in Laurie.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:24.689
So why would he leave?

00:01:24.689 --> 00:01:28.880
It frankly doesn't make much sense since he
knew Laurie was still out there and he clearly

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.300
wants to put a knife in her.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.869
He looked like an unbeatable killing machine
at the end of "Halloween Kills" so if there

00:01:33.869 --> 00:01:37.649
was any time for him to have a final bout
with Laurie, it would've been then.

00:01:37.649 --> 00:01:41.020
Plus, it's not like he went somewhere to catch
his breath for a moment.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:44.810
He's literally been gone for years at this
point and still didn't look like he was going

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:46.299
after Laurie any time soon.

00:01:46.299 --> 00:01:50.329
Sure, he looked like he was in a weakened
state when Corey finds him, but come on, this

00:01:50.329 --> 00:01:52.439
is Michael Myers we're talking about here.

00:01:52.439 --> 00:01:55.100
He's not the type to leave things unfinished.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:58.990
While the original continuity saw Michael
motivated to kill Laurie because of their

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:02.090
familial connection, this trilogy doesn't
have that.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:06.190
All this trilogy offers is speculation in
"Kills" about how Michael keeps returning

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:07.549
to his childhood home.

00:02:07.549 --> 00:02:12.500
Yet there's no motivation ever established
for why he goes after his victims specifically,

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:16.000
so it needs to be asked, why does Michael
care so much about Laurie?

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.330
Is it just because she's the one that got
away?

00:02:18.330 --> 00:02:22.180
Does he see her as the ultimate kill because
of how strong she's been against him?

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:23.870
Honestly, what's Michael's deal?

00:02:23.870 --> 00:02:26.550
There's actually a lot about Michael that's
left unanswered.

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:31.330
How is he able to withstand all the stabbings,
beatings, and damaging blows he's taken throughout

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:32.330
the years?

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:36.120
He literally gets stabbed in the heart by
Laurie in the film's final battle and then

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:38.810
suddenly rises to nearly choke her to death.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:42.530
What is it about Michael that's made him more
than a normal man for so many years?

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:46.470
The film never provides that answer and leaves
Michael as ambiguous as ever.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:50.330
It's a choice that makes sense given that
Michael is always meant to simply be this

00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:53.200
malevolent evil entity with unclear motives.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:57.190
But with this being Laurie and Michael's final
bout, it feels like a missed opportunity to

00:02:57.190 --> 00:02:59.700
truly understand The Shape.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.880
Once Corey decides to give into his more violent
side, there's almost nothing that seems capable

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:04.880
of stopping him.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:08.660
He enacts some kills that are nearly as brutal
as, if not more than, Michael's, and even

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:11.489
brings the big guy along for a tag-team murder
match.

00:03:11.489 --> 00:03:15.950
Yet it's not hard to see why Corey is near
unstoppable, since the police are often nowhere

00:03:15.950 --> 00:03:16.980
to be found.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.871
Throughout the "Halloween" franchise, the
Haddonfield police are never too incompetent,

00:03:19.871 --> 00:03:24.590
and would at least notice that people are
either disappearing or being brutally killed.

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:28.650
Here though, there's barely a mention that
these people have disappeared, and Corey rarely

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:31.900
runs into any obstacles since the police don't
do too much.

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:36.000
No one even questions if Michael is back given
the string of disappearances or that it's

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:37.970
all happening around Halloween.

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:41.060
Maybe they're off dealing with a different
violent occurrence in this darker depiction

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:42.090
of Haddonfield.

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:46.280
But how did no one really become aware that
Corey's rampage was going on?

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:49.590
When Corey first comes across Michael Myers
while he's hiding in the sewers, he's not

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.550
exactly in the best of conditions.

00:03:51.550 --> 00:03:55.269
He's clearly weakened from the big fight at
the end of "Halloween Kills," but he quickly

00:03:55.269 --> 00:03:58.700
regains his strength after getting his first
kill with the help of Corey.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:02.510
So if Michael is ready to get back out there
and start killing again, why is he basically

00:04:02.510 --> 00:04:06.760
tied to the hip with Corey and only seems
to come out after he shows up?

00:04:06.760 --> 00:04:10.750
Seriously, Michael only leaves the sewer if
Corey ends up coming and picking him up on

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.650
his motorcycle, which could've been a hilarious
visual to see.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:15.980
Does he have stage fright now or something?

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:20.100
Even on Halloween day, he just sits in his
sewer hideout until Corey comes by to steal

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:24.120
his mask, and it's just odd to see Michael
not go back to his killing routine once he's

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:25.320
got his strength back.

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:29.530
It's legitimately one of the strangest things
about Michael in "Halloween Ends" and sadly

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:33.720
keeps him off-screen for longer than any fan
would expect or want.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:38.040
Once Corey and Michael meet, they start to
work together and amass a decent body count.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:41.759
Corey seems to help Michael regain his strength
while Michael gives Corey the power to tap

00:04:41.759 --> 00:04:43.080
into his evil side.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:47.220
However, you can't help but wonder by the
end who was actually in charge in this dynamic.

00:04:47.220 --> 00:04:50.850
For the most part, it would seem like Corey
has control since he seems slightly stronger

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:54.320
than Michael for most of the movie and is
clearly leading the kills.

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:57.070
However, perhaps Michael was conning us all
along.

00:04:57.070 --> 00:05:01.010
Corey's plan was to see him go on a destruction
spree through Haddonfield before going off

00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:02.820
into the sunset with Allyson.

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:06.160
So it doesn't really seem like his dying on
the floor of Laurie's house was a part of

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:07.160
the plan.

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:08.900
So maybe this was Michael's plan all along.

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:13.070
He did follow Corey to Laurie's house after
Corey took his mask, and it does lead to the

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:15.420
big finale fight between him and Laurie.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:18.979
Michael Myers has never seemed like much of
a planner in this franchise, but maybe he's

00:05:18.979 --> 00:05:21.229
gotten wise in his old age.

00:05:21.229 --> 00:05:25.271
Laurie and Frank's awkward but adorable relationship
in "Halloween Kills" was one of that film's

00:05:25.271 --> 00:05:29.280
biggest highlights and ends up being a very
sweet part of "Halloween Ends."

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:33.080
There's nothing more heartwarming than seeing
their natural chemistry flourish, and you

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:35.259
desperately want these two to be together.

00:05:35.259 --> 00:05:39.419
After dealing with Michael Myers for so many
years, Laurie deserves some love in her life.

00:05:39.419 --> 00:05:40.560
So do they end up together?

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:44.060
Well, they don't share an on-screen kiss or
anything, and we don't see them boarding a

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:46.560
plane to Japan as they talk about during the
movie.

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.770
However, it's a pretty safe bet that they
do get together after the events of the film

00:05:50.770 --> 00:05:54.490
since their final moment together on Laurie's
porch is fairly emotional.

00:05:54.490 --> 00:05:57.991
There's no definitive answer to whether or
not Laurie and Frank end up together, but

00:05:57.991 --> 00:06:00.380
the film does make you believe that it is
possible.

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:04.870
With the traumatic history that Allyson has
endured in Haddonfield with both her parents

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:08.840
and friends being slain by Michael Myers,
it's not too shocking to hear that she's looking

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:09.840
to leave.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:14.030
While Laurie is able to keep her there through
guilt and concern, Allyson does find her opportunity

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:17.130
to leave when she meets Corey and he suggests
doing so.

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:20.949
While her original plans to leave with Corey
fall through, Allyson does end up leaving

00:06:20.949 --> 00:06:22.840
Haddonfield before the credits roll.

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:25.160
The only question is, where is Allyson going?

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:29.259
Truthfully, we'll probably never know the
answer, as the scene of her leaving Haddonfield

00:06:29.259 --> 00:06:32.220
is likely just to symbolize that she's leaving
the town for good.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:34.560
Maybe, though, there can be a more intriguing
answer.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.539
It's unclear whether or not "Halloween Ends"
will really be the end of the franchise.

00:06:38.539 --> 00:06:42.620
And maybe this scene will lead to a follow-up
film or trilogy keeping up with Allyson as

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:46.650
she discovers new Halloween horrors as she
tries to start a new life.

00:06:46.650 --> 00:06:50.270
In the final shot of "Halloween Ends," the
camera moves throughout Laurie's house as

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:53.700
a homage to the final scene of the original
Halloween film.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:06:59.030
However, there is one item that sticks out
in the final room, Michael's mask.

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:05.250
You can't help but wonder why didn't Laurie
destroy the mask when she pushed Michael's

00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:06.840
body into the grinder.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:10.870
It's kind of odd that she didn't destroy it,
since Michael's mask is a key identifier of

00:07:10.870 --> 00:07:14.500
him and a major symbol of the fear he's spread
across Haddonfield.

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:17.979
Maybe she needed it as inspiration to finish
the book she's been writing throughout the

00:07:17.979 --> 00:07:22.040
film or just simply wanted it because she
wasn't completely over him even after killing

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:23.040
him.

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.870
Perhaps too, this could be a hint that someone
else could pick up Michael's mask and start

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:27.410
a new reign of terror in Haddonfield.

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:31.621
It's not too surprising to see "Halloween
Ends" definitively kill Michael Myers, it's

00:07:31.621 --> 00:07:34.630
just strange that his mask couldn't go with
him.

00:07:34.630 --> 00:07:39.210
Michael Myers is dead by the end of "Halloween
Ends" and Laurie seemingly has moved on.

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:42.190
So it would be pretty easy to see this as
the last movie.

00:07:42.190 --> 00:07:46.710
But the film is a little shaky with its conclusiveness,
creating some avenues for the franchise to

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:52.400
return.

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:55.740
Michael's mask just sitting on the table leaves
the impression that someone else could pick

00:07:55.740 --> 00:07:57.460
it up to continue his legacy.

00:07:57.460 --> 00:08:01.539
It's extremely unlikely that this franchise
will continue under David Gordon Green and

00:08:01.539 --> 00:08:03.110
Blumhouse Productions.

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:05.849
Green said that this entry is the conclusion
of his story.

00:08:05.849 --> 00:08:10.630
Blumhouse CEO Jason Blum added that the company
has no more rights to make more "Halloween"

00:08:10.630 --> 00:08:11.660
films either.

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:15.400
But could the franchise continue under different
hands, even after Michael's death?

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:19.100
The only thing that's harder to kill than
Michael Myers is a lucrative franchise.

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:23.030
We've already seen that the franchise is willing
to bring in other killers besides Michael,

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:27.139
with Corey mainly acting as this film's killer
while Michael hides in the sewers.

00:08:27.139 --> 00:08:31.020
And though Laurie is likely gone from the
franchise for good, longtime fans know that

00:08:31.020 --> 00:08:32.750
she hasn't always led the films.

00:08:32.750 --> 00:08:36.750
For example, Jamie Lloyd led a couple of films
and "Halloween: Resurrection" saw a whole

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:39.979
new cast take on Michael after Laurie is killed.

00:08:39.979 --> 00:08:42.810
"Why the hell are you dressed like me anyway?

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:44.000
I ain't paying you to be Michael Myers.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:46.120
I'm playing Michael Myers."

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:50.421
"Halloween Ends" might be the end of this
trilogy and the franchise as we know it, but

00:08:50.421 --> 00:08:54.480
even while it emphasizes the "End" in the
title during the credits, it doesn't feel

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:55.579
totally conclusive.

